content.thinking:
Wait, the problem is deeper. `loadApp()` sets `this.window` and `this.document` to new instances. But `disposeApp()` is async and sets `this.window = undefined`. So `this.document` is accessed after `disposeApp()` has already been called. Let me look at the world.js again more carefully to understand the async behavior.

Looking at world.js:
```js
async disposeApp() {
    const w = this.window;
    if (!w) return;
    this.window = undefined;
    this.document = undefined;
    if (w.happyDOM && typeof w.happyDOM.close === 'function') { await w.happyDOM.close(); return; }
    if (w.happyDOM && typeof w.happyDOM.abort === 'function') { await w.happyDOM.abort(); return; }
    if (typeof w.close === 'function') w.close();
  }
```

So `disposeApp()` sets `this.window = undefined` and `this.document = undefined` at the start, then awaits `happyDOM.close()`. The problem is that after `loadApp()` runs, `this.document` and `this.window` are recreated. But the subsequent steps run before the `.then()` completes.

The fix with `return` should work - returning the promise makes Cucumber wait. Let me run the persistence scenarios to test.

